fact WorkItem(id: "WI-893", description: some(value: "A `[simp]` ATTRIBUTE IS SILENTLY EATEN WHEN A COMMENT LINE PRECEDES THE RULE ENTRY — a GLR tie in `tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js` drops the attribute and turns it into a SEPARATE JUNK RULE, with no diagnostic. Found by a review agent while driving WI-887, and it is what made that ticket's central measurement an ARTIFACT.\nMEASURED, by `npx tree-sitter parse` against the checked-in `src/parser.c`. Inside a `rule { }` block, an entry carrying `[simp]` parses correctly — UNLESS a comment line immediately precedes it, in which case the parse yields `rule_entry [n,4]-[n,38]` with NO `meta_block`, followed by a SECOND `rule_entry [n,39]-[n,45]` whose head is a `collection_literal` — the `[simp]` text re-parsed as a new rule headed by the list `[simp]`. Bisected to exactly that trigger: `(entry, entry [simp], entry [simp])` yields TWO meta_blocks and the rules fire; `(entry, --comment, entry [simp], entry [simp])` yields ONE meta_block plus one collection_literal. Confirmed on the real file both ways: `bool.anthill` with a `-- if-then-else` line above `ite_true` parses ONE meta_block; delete that one comment line and it parses TWO.\nTHE CONFLICT IS DECLARED: `tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js`:52-53 names exactly this pair. This is the WI-448 hazard in a new production pair, and the remedy there was `prec.dynamic` (recorded as the `tree-sitter-glr-comment-tie` lesson: tree-sitter `extras` — comments — are NAMED, so they participate in the tie).\nWHY IT IS WORSE THAN A PARSE QUIRK: it is SILENT and it produces a WRONG PROGRAM rather than an error. The attribute vanishes, the equation becomes inert (WI-881: `[simp]` is the ENABLEMENT), and a junk fact is asserted — stdlib load goes 2362 facts with the comments, 2361 without. Nothing warns. The repo's standing principle is a loud error over a silent skip, and this is a silent skip in the PARSER, upstream of every check.\nTHE DAMAGE IT ALREADY DID, and why this is filed rather than fixed inline: driving WI-887 I tagged `bool.anthill`'s two `ite` case laws `[simp]`, and a `-- if-then-else` comment sat immediately above the FIRST one. So `ite_true`'s tag was eaten and `ite_false`'s survived. I then probed exactly `Bool.ite(true, ...)` — the one branch whose tag was gone — measured `OperationBodyMissing`, and concluded that tagging buys nothing. It buys everything: with the comment removed so both tags parse, and the operation DECLARED, `Bool.ite(true, 10, 20)` -> 10 and `Bool.ite(false, 10, 20)` -> 20. A half-eaten pair of case laws is the \"half-backed looks backed\" failure WI-887's own prose warns about, arriving from the parser.\nSWEEP: a review agent parsed EVERY `.anthill` file in the repo (stdlib, anthill-stl, cpp-gen, examples, anthill-todo) and found exactly ONE dropped attribute — the one my WI-887 edit introduced, now reverted. So the shipped tree is CLEAN today and this is a latent trap, not an outstanding corruption. That is precisely why it needs a test: the next author to comment a `[simp]` law will hit it, and nothing will tell them.\nACCEPTANCE: a comment line immediately preceding a `[simp]`-tagged `rule { }` entry parses with the `meta_block` attached (tree-sitter corpus case, BOTH orders, plus the no-comment control); a RUST load-level test asserts the rule is indexed as a `[simp]` equation and FIRES, since corpus tests do not run under `cargo test` and this defect is invisible to a green suite; the junk `collection_literal`-headed rule no longer appears (assert the stdlib fact count is unchanged by adding or removing a comment above a tagged law); the parser is regenerated (`npx tree-sitter generate`) and the generated file committed; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. CONSIDER, and answer explicitly: whether a rule head that is a bare `collection_literal` is EVER legal — if not, refusing it at load is a cheap second line of defence that would have made this loud. REFERENCE: WI-448 / the `prec.dynamic` remedy, WI-881 (`[simp]` is the enablement, so a dropped tag is a dropped definition), WI-887 (the ticket whose measurement this invalidated)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T10:25:35Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-893", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The GLR tie is broken by `prec.dynamic(1, $.meta_block)` on `rule_entry`'s trailing meta_block — the WI-448 remedy in the second production pair that has this hazard. MEASURED both ways, and the pre-fix measurement is the acceptance: with the original grammar restored, `(entry, --comment, entry [simp], entry [simp])` yields ONE meta_block + one collection_literal; with the fix, TWO. Line comment and block comment trip it alike, and in EITHER position of a tagged pair (a comment above the SECOND entry eats that one's tag) — the ticket's bisection only covered the first.\n\nWHY `prec.dynamic` AND NOT A GRAMMAR RESTRICTION, since the ticket did not choose: the alternatives were driven and are worse. Restricting what a head may START with needs a `_head_goal` forking `_atom_term`, which the grammar's own WI-312 comment argues against (\"keeping it as a shared rule guarantees the receiver set never drifts\"), and it would refuse `rule [?a] = ?b`, which the load rule below PERMITS — a stricter rule than the one stated, and a disagreement to maintain. A separator between entries is source-breaking and needs an external scanner this grammar does not have. Decisive: the defect is a tree-sitter GLR ARTIFACT, not a language defect — `scaland`'s fastparse port has the same juxtaposed `ruleEntry.rep` and never had the bug (PEG, ordered choice), so the other two options would change the LANGUAGE and force a matching change in a port that was never broken. The bias also loses nothing expressible: DRIVEN, `rule { p(?x) [?a] = ?b }` still parses as two entries, because `[?a]` is not meta-block-shaped (`meta_entry` needs a `name` key).\n\nNO SIBLING PRODUCTION HAS THIS TIE — checked, not assumed. The hazard needs the NEXT member of a `repeat` to be able to begin with `[`; all 15 `optional($.meta_block)` sites and all 11 repeats were enumerated, and every other member is keyword-led or name-led (`operation_entry` starts `optional(visibility) name`). `rule_entry` is the only `[`-startable sibling, which is why `[$.rule_entry]` is the only such declared conflict.\n\nTHE ANSWER TO THE \"CONSIDER\": a bare `collection_literal` head is NEVER legal, and neither is any other bare literal. MEASURED first: `rule { [simp] }` LOADED CLEAN before this change, which is what kept the defect silent end to end. Refused now at `convert_rule_heads`, the function that already owns this class (`let_binding`, `cut`), for a reason independent of the accident — a head is a CONCLUSION, a literal denotes a VALUE, and a value is not a proposition. It is not merely a second line of defence: it is what makes the `prec.dynamic` bias EXACT rather than arbitrary tie-breaking, since the reading the bias discards is one nothing may write. Scope is the whole literal family (scalars + `[…]`/`{…}`/`(…)`), because the argument does not distinguish `[simp]` from `42`; enumerating only the accident's shape would be a gap list wearing a rule's clothes.\n\nTHE REFUSAL WAS A HALF-FIX AT FIRST, found by the review's altitude agent: `fact` is the OTHER producer of a conclusion, and CLAUDE.md's own \"facts are rules — a fact is a rule with empty body\" makes the rule apply there. DRIVEN: `fact 42`, `fact [simp]`, `fact \"s\"` all parsed and reached `assert_fact` — the identical junk-entry outcome, on the surface a user is likelier to hand-write. Both producers now go through one owner, `reject_literal_conclusion`.\n\nTESTS. Corpus (`test/corpus/rule_entry_meta.txt`): four cases — no-comment control, line comment above the first tagged entry, above the second, and a block comment — sharing one body so each differs from the control by exactly one comment line. Rust (`wi893_comment_eats_simp_test.rs`), because NOTHING in the repo runs `npx tree-sitter test` under `cargo test`: two body-less operations whose `[simp]` laws are their whole definition, one with a comment above its THEN law and one above its ELSE law, all four branches driven on one interpreter. That asymmetry is the point — pre-fix exactly two of the four died `OperationBodyMissing` while their siblings answered, so asserting one branch per operation would have passed against the defect half the time, which is precisely how WI-887 was misled. Plus the differential fact/rule count (the ticket's own instrument, on a small program rather than the stdlib, since the stdlib has no such comment to add or remove today; the control half is DERIVED by stripping comment lines so the two cannot drift), and the refusal + its atom-heads control. VERIFIED PRE-FIX: with the original grammar restored, the two load-level tests FAIL, and they fail LOUDLY through the new refusal rather than silently — which is the second line of defence doing exactly what it was added for.\n\nTHE ACCEPTANCE ITEM THAT DOES NOT APPLY: \"the parser is regenerated and the generated file committed\" — `tree-sitter-anthill/src/` is GITIGNORED in this repo, and `build.rs` regenerates `parser.c` whenever `grammar.js` is newer. Nothing to commit; regenerated and verified live (the built artifact is byte-identical to a fresh regeneration of the post-change grammar).\n\nMEASURED COST OF THE FIX: table-neutral. `parser.c` is 2,926,093 bytes both ways; STATE_COUNT / SYMBOL_COUNT / PRODUCTION_ID_COUNT / TOKEN_COUNT / FIELD_COUNT all identical; the entire delta is 13 action entries changing `REDUCE(sym_rule_entry, N, 0, P)` to `REDUCE(sym_rule_entry, N, 1, P)`. No new conflict is introduced — `[$.rule_entry]` already existed, and dynamic precedence is consulted only when versions are already being compared. Stdlib parse time indistinguishable across 3 runs each.\n\nALSO: `bool.anthill`'s \"Keep the tags clear of a preceding comment line — see WI-893\" is DELETED — the constraint no longer exists, and a library must not carry a workaround for a fixed defect. The rule went to `docs/kernel-language.md` §\"A head is an atom\" (and says convert-time, not load-time — the WI-850 distinction); CLAUDE.md deliberately untouched.\n\nADJACENT, NOT FILED, because it is documented design and not a defect: an operation's TRAILING `[...]` is absorbed into the return type as an application — DRIVEN, `operation get_values(self: GPS) -> Vec3 [Vec3FromConstDoublePtr3]` silently becomes `Vec3[Vec3FromConstDoublePtr3]`. `grammar.js`'s `meta_clause` comment states this explicitly and the `meta` keyword is the disambiguating vehicle provided for it. Different mechanism (static `prec(1)`, no comment, no GLR tie), and no repo file uses the absorbed spelling.\n\nREVIEW: `/code-review` (4 agents). The altitude agent found the `fact` half-fix and that the refusal is the bias's justification rather than mere defence; the reuse agent found that `parse_rule_body_goals_ir` already was the unconstrained-term harness the parse tests needed (so `parse_rule_head_ir` became a thin `parse_term_ir` over it, rather than a new probe-wrapper twin) and that `is_data_literal_kind` should be folded into `is_term_kind` so the two lists cannot drift; the efficiency agent caught that the file's \"STDLIB LOADS: TWO\" header counted THREE. Full workspace suite GREEN via `rustland/scripts/test.sh`; tree-sitter corpus 190/190.", at: "2026-07-31T10:25:33Z")
